5. Step-by-Step Explanation

Step 1: Customer opens the e-commerce website
-----------------------------------------------
The customer opens the website using a browser.

For local Kubernetes testing, we used:
>> http://localhost:8080
This address works while the Kubernetes port-forward command is running.

The browser sends an HTTP request to the application.

Step 2: Kubernetes forwards the request
----------------------------------------------
Our Ecommerce UI is deployed inside the ecommerce namespace.

The Kubernetes Service is named:

ecommerce-ui
The Service listens on port 80 and forwards traffic to the Ecommerce UI Pods on port 4000.

          Browser
             |
             v
      localhost:8080
             |
             v
      Kubernetes Service
       ecommerce-ui:80
             |
             v
       Ecommerce UI Pod
          Port 4000

Step 3: Express receives the request
-------------------------------------------------
The Node.js application runs the Express gateway.

The main application file is:
server/server.js

Express receives the HTTP request.
When the customer requests the homepage, Express serves the compiled React application.

Step 4: React displays the website
------------------------------------------------
The browser downloads the React application's HTML, JavaScript, and CSS files.

React renders the user interface.

For example, the customer can see the navigation bar, product listing area, login page, and other frontend components.

Step 5: React requests product information
-------------------------------------------------
When the customer opens the products page, React needs product information.
The frontend sends an HTTP request to the Express gateway.
The gateway identifies the matching product route.

Step 6: Gateway communicates with Product Catalog
-------------------------------------------------
The gateway reads the Product Catalog URL from its environment configuration.

In Kubernetes, we configured:

>> PRODUCT_API_URL=http://product-catalog:3001
The gateway uses the Fetch API to communicate with Product Catalog.

The request travels through the internal Kubernetes network.

       React Frontend
              |
              v
        Express Gateway
              |
              | HTTP Request
              v
       Product Catalog
          Port 3001
              |
              v
           MongoDB
              |
              v
       Product Information

Step 7: Product Catalog retrieves the data
----------------------------------------------------
Product Catalog queries MongoDB.
MongoDB returns the product information to Product Catalog.
Product Catalog then sends the response back to the Ecommerce UI gateway.

Step 8: Gateway returns the response
----------------------------------------------------
The Express gateway receives the backend response and forwards the relevant information to the frontend.

The response may contain product details such as:

{
  "id": 1,
  "name": "Laptop",
  "price": 50000,
  "category": "Electronics"
}

This is an illustrative product response; the actual fields depend on the Product Catalog API.

Step 9: React displays the products
----------------------------------------------------
React receives the API response.
It uses the product information to display product cards, names, prices, and other details.

The customer can now browse the products.


